WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.560
A nerve spike is over almost at once.

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.880
Excite a ventricular heart cell, and it stays depolarized roughly a hundred times as

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.460
long. What holds it up there?

4
00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:17.793
Picture one ventricular cell from a human heart.

5
00:00:18.873 --> 00:00:22.893
At rest its voltage sits flat, near minus ninety millivolts.

6
00:00:23.953 --> 00:00:26.913
A potassium current called IK1 holds it there.

7
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:31.923
Now sodium channels open, and the voltage shoots up past zero.

8
00:00:33.243 --> 00:00:38.223
Those channels inactivate within milliseconds, and a brief outward potassium current

9
00:00:38.223 --> 00:00:39.323
cuts a small notch.

10
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.020
Yet the voltage does not fall back.

11
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.980
It hangs near zero for about two hundred milliseconds.

12
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.020
This long stretch is the plateau.

13
00:00:55.183 --> 00:00:57.843
Two sets of channels pull against each other.

14
00:00:59.143 --> 00:01:01.923
L-type calcium channels let calcium flow in.

15
00:01:03.223 --> 00:01:06.963
Delayed-rectifier potassium channels let potassium flow out.

16
00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:13.867
Here is the one idea.

17
00:01:15.127 --> 00:01:16.547
The plateau is a near tie.

18
00:01:17.947 --> 00:01:22.867
Inward and outward currents almost cancel, so the voltage changes only slowly,

19
00:01:23.507 --> 00:01:25.607
drifting down as the outward currents gain.

20
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:35.043
But at rest, a large potassium conductance held this cell near minus ninety.

21
00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:38.703
Why doesn't it drag the voltage straight back down?

22
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:42.513
Because IK1 is an inward rectifier.

23
00:01:43.713 --> 00:01:48.153
It passes current well near rest, but largely shuts at plateau voltages.

24
00:01:49.513 --> 00:01:53.253
So total conductance is low, and small currents hold the voltage.

25
00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:01.897
So this balance is easy to disturb.

26
00:02:03.557 --> 00:02:05.817
Block just one of the outward potassium currents.

27
00:02:07.017 --> 00:02:08.217
What happens to the plateau?

28
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:20.743
It lengthens. Drugs that block the potassium channel called hERG do this.

29
00:02:21.983 --> 00:02:27.403
The longer plateau reaches the skin too, as a longer QT interval on the ECG.

30
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.680
Losing one current is partly covered, though.

31
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.020
Several potassium currents can repolarize the cell.

32
00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.000
That margin is called the repolarization reserve.

33
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.020
How does the tie finally break?

34
00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:54.740
The L-type channels inactivate, shutting with time at depolarized voltages, and as

35
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.280
calcium builds up inside.

36
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.780
Calcium's pull fades, and the potassium currents win.

37
00:03:01.833 --> 00:03:07.033
As the voltage falls, IK1 conducts again and finishes repolarization.

38
00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:12.653
The cell returns to where it started, flat near minus ninety millivolts.

39
00:03:19.017 --> 00:03:21.297
So the long hold takes no huge current.

40
00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:27.357
It takes opposing pulls, closely matched, while the resting potassium path is

41
00:03:27.357 --> 00:03:31.337
largely shut. When the match ends, the plateau ends.
